From: Maksim Kruzeman <kruzeman@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: quiet the repeated handover warning

The SM6115 ADSP image keeps re-asserting the SMP2P "handover" item after
the first handover (~2 Hz on the Lenovo Tab P11), so the guard in
q6v5_handover_interrupt() fired continuously at dev_err level. Nothing is
broken - the remote keeps running and audio, Wi-Fi and charging work - but
the flood fills dmesg within minutes and hides real errors.

Log it at debug level instead: the condition is expected with the vendor
firmware this port runs.
diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
index 58d5b85e5..86ad2c085 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -165,7 +165,14 @@ static irqreturn_t q6v5_handover_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		/*
+		 * Some vendor remote-processor images re-assert the SMP2P handover
+		 * item after the first handover (the SM6115 ADSP does it roughly
+		 * twice a second), and every re-assertion used to print here. The
+		 * condition is harmless - the remote keeps running - but the flood
+		 * flushes dmesg and hides real problems, so rate-limit it.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
